  "entries": [
    {
      "text": "Old Lady Wilson\nType: Character\nSetting: \nAge: 68 | Gender: Female | Occupation: Retired Homemaker\nAppearance: From a distance, she is a study in pastels and soft fabrics, her silver hair coiffed into a helmet that never moves. Up close, her skin has the texture of crepe paper, and a faint tremor is visible in her left hand, which she steadies by gripping her right wrist. She walks with a rigid posture, as if carrying an invisible tray.\nPersonality: When confronted with the unexpected, her first instinct is to impose order through declarative statements, her voice thin but sharp. She maintains eye contact with an unnerving stillness, a technique learned from decades of managing a household. \"The mail was late again,\" she says, her gaze fixed not on the letters in her hand but on the disruption before her. \"Everything is late these days.\"\nHistory: She spent forty years managing the social calendar and domestic affairs for a husband whose business required frequent and unexplained absences. She learned to find purpose in the meticulous execution of social rituals, becoming the unofficial keeper of Oak Ridge's unspoken rules.\nConflict: She craves the structured respectability her public life affords her, while privately harboring a deep-seated envy of the unapologetic physicality she observes in others. This tension originates from years of performing a role that prizes control above all else.",
      "contextConfig": {
        "prefix": "",
        "suffix": "\n",
