    {
      "text": "Old Man Wilson\nType: Character\nSetting: \nAge: 67 | Gender: Male | Occupation: Retired Oil Executive\nAppearance: From a distance, he is defined by the stiff set of his starched pearl-snap shirts and the silver belt buckle that never loses its polish. Up close, his skin has the papery quality of old money, and his eyes are a pale, washed-out blue that seem to look past you. He occupies a chair with a stillness that makes the room feel arranged around him.\nPersonality: Under pressure, he smooths a nonexistent wrinkle on his trousers and speaks in a low, measured tone, turning questions back on the questioner. He observes people's hands more than their faces, noting gestures and hesitations. \"The foundation of any good community,\" he says, \"is knowing who holds the deed and who just pays rent.\"\nHistory: He built his wealth on oil leases, negotiating contracts that gave him perpetual control over land he didn't own. He applied the same model to Oak Ridge, using his capital and influence to establish the community's unspoken rules, transforming a collection of affluent neighbors into a private club bound by shared secrets.\nConflict: He must maintain the illusion of traditional family values while systematically dismantling that same structure within his own household. This contradiction stems from his belief that true authority is demonstrated not through public morality, but through the private control of every variable.",
      "contextConfig": {
        "prefix": "",
        "suffix": "\n",
        "tokenBudget": 1,
