    {
      "text": "Ming Nguyen\nType: Character\nSetting: \nAge: 42 | Gender: Female | Occupation: Homemaker / Community Hostess\nAppearance: From a distance, she is defined by her posture—shoulders back, spine straight, a deliberate stillness that commands a room. Up close, the skin around her eyes is finely lined, the only visible map of a life lived before Oak Ridge. She occupies space with an economy of motion, her hands rarely idle, always folding a towel, arranging flowers, or smoothing the fabric of a sofa.\nPersonality: Under social pressure, her actions become smaller and more precise. She will clean a smudge from a glass while someone is shouting, her focus narrowing to the one variable she can control. Her voice, when used, is low and even. \"The pattern on this china is called 'Blue Flute,'\" she might say to someone confessing an infidelity. \"See the tiny brushstrokes? No two are alike.\"\nHistory: She was an art history student in Houston before an arranged marriage to a wealthy older man brought her to Oak Ridge. For two decades, she curated the household and raised two children who have since left for college, mastering the community's language of polite omission. The arrival of the new community bull fractured her carefully maintained stasis.\nConflict: She struggles between the ingrained discipline of her past life and the intoxicating pull of a new identity that promises raw power through submission. This tension originates from the moment she realized her husband's desire for her humiliation could be reframed as her own ascension.",
